Q. What are the core concepts of JPA? 
============================================================

JPA has several core concepts used to manage Java objects
and their database persistence.

Important concepts include:

      • Entity
      • EntityManager
      • Persistence Context
      • Criteria API
      • JPQL (Java Persistence Query Language)
      • Persistence Unit

1. PERSISTENCE
------------------------------------------------------------

Persistence means storing the state of a Java object
permanently in a database so that the data remains available
even after the application or JVM stops.

Example:

    Employee object
          ↓
       persist
          ↓
    Employee table

So:

    Java Object → Database = Persistence

------------------------------------------------------------
2. PERSISTENCE UNIT
------------------------------------------------------------

A Persistence Unit is a logical group of configuration
information that defines how JPA should connect to and manage
a particular database.

It typically defines:

    → Database connection details
    → JPA provider (e.g., Hibernate)
    → Entity classes
    → JPA properties

Traditionally, it is configured in:

    META-INF/persistence.xml

------------------------------------------------------------
SIMPLE FLOW
------------------------------------------------------------

    Persistence Unit
          ↓
    JPA Configuration
          ↓
    EntityManagerFactory
          ↓
    EntityManager
          ↓
    Entity
          ↓
    Database

------------------------------------------------------------
INTERVIEW SHORT ANSWER
------------------------------------------------------------

"JPA's core concepts include Entity, EntityManager,
Persistence Context, Persistence Unit, EntityTransaction,
JPQL and mapping annotations.

Persistence means storing the state of Java objects
permanently in a database.

A Persistence Unit is a logical set of JPA configuration
that defines the database, JPA provider, entities and
persistence properties."
